# C++

> **Public documentation only.** The implementation repository remains private. These guides describe the wiki's 0.3.1 interface; published package versions and platform support may differ. Source-checkout/build steps require separately authorized source access. Examples use dummy credentials.

C++17. Link `RedXAI::red-xai-encrypt` after `find_package(red-xai-encrypt)`. Header: `<redxai/encrypt.hpp>`.

This API writes a **binary** `.Red_SecretXAI`. `to_image`, `to_binary`, `rerender`, `split_images`, `join_images`, `split_port_key`, and `restore_from_cards` throw `available in the Python SDK only … until v0.4`. Use [convert](Red-X-PNG.md). A PNG input raises an error that names the Python package.

## Password mode

```cpp
#include <redxai/encrypt.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("restored");
  auto out = redxai::encrypt_file(
      "notes.txt", "correct horse battery staple", redxai::Mode::Password);
  std::cout << redxai::decrypt_file(out, "correct horse battery staple", "restored") << "\n";

  auto token = redxai::encrypt_text(
      "hello", "correct horse battery staple", redxai::Mode::Password);
  std::cout << redxai::decrypt_text(token, "correct horse battery staple") << "\n";
}
```

Default mode is `redxai::Mode::Port`.

## Errors

`redxai::WrongPasswordError`, `NotRedSecretError`, `FileExistsError`, `UnsafeFilenameError`, `SetupDeclinedError`, `NativeLibraryError`. All derive from `redxai::Error`.

## Optional calls

```cpp
redxai::encrypt_file("notes.txt", "pw-2026", redxai::Mode::Port,
                     "/media/usb/.red-xai-port", [] { return true; });

redxai::encrypt_folder("photos", "pw-2026", "photos.Red_SecretXAI",
                       redxai::Mode::Port, "", [] { return true; });
std::filesystem::create_directories("photos-restored");
redxai::decrypt_folder("photos.Red_SecretXAI", "pw-2026", "photos-restored");

redxai::encrypt_drive("disk.img", "pw-2026", "disk.Red_SecretXAI");
redxai::decrypt_drive("disk.Red_SecretXAI", "pw-2026", "disk-restored.img");

auto blob = redxai::duress_seal(
    std::vector<std::string>{"real-pass", "everyday-pass"},
    std::vector<std::vector<uint8_t>>{{'r','e','a','l'}, {'c','o','v','e','r'}},
    8, 0);
auto opened = redxai::duress_open("everyday-pass", blob.data(), blob.size(), 8);

auto preview = redxai::archive_preview("photos.Red_SecretXAI", "pw-2026");
auto hw = redxai::hw_token_ex();
std::cout << redxai::hw_provider_name() << " " << redxai::hw_provider_strength() << "\n";
```

`encrypt_folder` throws if the mode is not port. Progress is `std::function<void(uint64_t done, uint64_t total_or_0)>`. Cancel is a `volatile int *` set to `1`. Device restore passes `RSX_ARCH_WRITE_DEVICE` and the device path as `confirm_device_name`.

A series of messages, opened in order. Both sides use the same 32-byte master. The salt pointer may be null.

```cpp
uint8_t master[32];
redxai::Ratchet tx(master), rx(master);
const uint8_t frame[] = "frame 0";
auto sealed = tx.seal(frame, sizeof frame);
auto plain = rx.open(sealed.data(), sealed.size());
```

Fill `master` from your own random source before that snippet. Share it once, out of band.

Hardware-bound keymaps use `rsx_session_create_hw` / `rsx_session_open_hw` from `rsx.h` (included by the easy header). See [Hardware binding](Hardware-binding.md).

More: [Port mode](Port-mode.md), [Folders, apps, and drives](Folders-apps-and-drives.md), [Decoy passwords](Decoy-passwords.md), [Recovery](Recovery.md).

# C#

> **Public documentation only.** The implementation repository remains private. These guides describe the wiki's 0.3.1 interface; published package versions and platform support may differ. Source-checkout/build steps require separately authorized source access. Examples use dummy credentials.

```bash
dotnet add package RedXAI.Encrypt
```

.NET 8. The package loads `librsx` for `linux-x64`, `linux-arm64`, and `win-x64`.

```csharp
using RedXAI.Encrypt;
```

This package writes a **binary** `.Red_SecretXAI`. `ToImage`, `ToBinary`, `Rerender`, `SplitImages`, `JoinImages`, `SplitPortKey`, `RestoreFromCards`, `CreatePort`, and `UnlockPort` throw `available in the Python SDK only … until v0.4`. Use [convert](Red-X-PNG.md) for the PNG. Opening a PNG raises an error that names the Python package.

## Password mode

```csharp
using RedXAI.Encrypt;

Directory.CreateDirectory("restored");
var outPath = RedSecret.EncryptFile("notes.txt", "correct horse battery staple", mode: "password");
Console.WriteLine(RedSecret.DecryptFile(outPath, "correct horse battery staple", outDir: "restored"));

var token = RedSecret.EncryptText("hello", "correct horse battery staple", mode: "password");
Console.WriteLine(RedSecret.DecryptText(token, "correct horse battery staple"));
```

## Errors

`WrongPasswordException`, `NotRedSecretException`, `OutputExistsException`, `UnsafeNameException`, `SetupDeclinedException`, `NativeLibraryException`. All derive from `RedXAIException`.

The wrong-password message is `Wrong password or the file was changed`. Pass `overwrite: true` when you intend to replace an existing output.

## Optional calls

```csharp
RedSecret.EncryptFile("notes.txt", "pw-2026", mode: "port",
    portPath: "/media/usb/.red-xai-port", onSetupPrompt: () => true);

RedSecret.EncryptFolder("photos", "pw-2026", "photos.Red_SecretXAI", onSetupPrompt: () => true);
Directory.CreateDirectory("photos-restored");
RedSecret.DecryptFolder("photos.Red_SecretXAI", "pw-2026", "photos-restored");

RedSecret.EncryptDrive("disk.img", "pw-2026", "disk.Red_SecretXAI", onSetupPrompt: () => true);
RedSecret.DecryptDrive("disk.Red_SecretXAI", "pw-2026", "disk-restored.img");

byte[] blob = RedSecret.DuressSeal(
    new[] { "real-pass", "everyday-pass" },
    new[] { Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("real"), Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("cover") },
    slots: 8, band: 0);
RedSecret.DuressOpen("everyday-pass", blob, slots: 8);

var (entries, summary) = RedSecret.ArchivePreview("photos.Red_SecretXAI", "pw-2026");
var hw = RedSecret.HwTokenEx();
Console.WriteLine(RedSecret.HwProviderName());
```

`EncryptFolder` / `DecryptFolder` / `EncryptDrive` / `DecryptDrive` take `progress` (`Action<ulong, ulong>`) and `cancel` (`CancellationToken`). Device restore: `flags: 8` and `confirmDeviceName` equal to the device path.

A series of messages, opened in order. Both sides use the same 32-byte master. Salt is 16 bytes or omitted.

```csharp
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;
using RedXAI.Encrypt;

byte[] master = RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes(32);
byte[] salt = RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes(16);
using var tx = new Ratchet(master, salt);
using var rx = new Ratchet(master, salt);
byte[] frame = tx.Seal(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("frame 0"));
Console.WriteLine(Encoding.UTF8.GetString(rx.Open(frame)));
```

More: [Port mode](Port-mode.md), [Folders, apps, and drives](Folders-apps-and-drives.md), [Hardware binding](Hardware-binding.md), [Decoy passwords](Decoy-passwords.md).

# Decoy passwords (optional)

> **Public documentation only.** The implementation repository remains private. These guides describe the wiki's 0.3.1 interface; published package versions and platform support may differ. Source-checkout/build steps require separately authorized source access. Examples use dummy credentials.

One sealed blob can hold more than one payload. Each password opens only its own payload. You choose how many slots to allocate (`n_slots` / `slots`, often 8) and a size band (`band`, `0` through `9`).

This is separate from [password-mode files](Seal-and-open.md). Build the blob, then [turn it into a red-X PNG](Red-X-PNG.md) if you want to send it as an image. `encode_image` / `convert` do not need the passwords.

If two passwords land on the same slot, Python raises `ParamError`. Draw again. The other SDKs return the same failure from the native call. Retry the seal.

## Python

```python
from redxai import _librsx as rsx
from redsecretsxai import encode_image, decode_image

def seal_decoy(passwords, payloads, n_slots=8, band=0):
    for _ in range(32):
        try:
            return rsx.duress_seal(passwords, payloads, n_slots=n_slots, band=band)
        except rsx.ParamError:
            continue
    raise RuntimeError("use more slots")

blob = seal_decoy(
    [b"real-pass", b"everyday-pass"],
    [b"the real plans", b"grocery list"],
)
encode_image(blob, "postcard.png")
print(rsx.duress_open(b"everyday-pass", decode_image("postcard.png"), n_slots=8))
print(rsx.duress_open(b"real-pass", decode_image("postcard.png"), n_slots=8))
```

A payload can itself be a decoy blob (nesting). Open the outer password first, then the inner one, with the same `n_slots` you sealed with.

`rsx.duress_size(band, n_slots)` returns the blob size before you seal.

## JavaScript

```js
const R = require('red-xai-encrypt');
const blob = R.duressSeal(
  ['real-pass', 'everyday-pass'],
  [Buffer.from('the real plans'), Buffer.from('grocery list')],
  { slots: 8, band: 0 },
);
console.log(R.duressOpen('everyday-pass', blob, { slots: 8 }).toString());
```

Image wrapping from JavaScript throws until v0.4. Save `blob` to a file and run `redsecretsxai convert` .

## C#

```csharp
using System.Text;
using RedXAI.Encrypt;
byte[] blob = RedSecret.DuressSeal(
    new[] { "real-pass", "everyday-pass" },
    new[] { Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("the real plans"), Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("grocery list") },
    slots: 8, band: 0);
Console.WriteLine(Encoding.UTF8.GetString(RedSecret.DuressOpen("everyday-pass", blob, slots: 8)));
```

## Java

```java
import com.redxai.encrypt.RedSecret;
byte[] blob = RedSecret.duressSeal(
    new String[] { "real-pass", "everyday-pass" },
    new byte[][] { "the real plans".getBytes(), "grocery list".getBytes() },
    8, 0);
System.out.println(new String(RedSecret.duressOpen("everyday-pass", blob, 8)));
```

## C++

```cpp
#include <redxai/encrypt.hpp>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

std::vector<std::string> passwords = {"real-pass", "everyday-pass"};
std::vector<std::vector<uint8_t>> payloads = {
    {'t','h','e',' ','r','e','a','l'},
    {'l','i','s','t'},
};
auto blob = redxai::duress_seal(passwords, payloads, /*n_slots=*/8, /*band=*/0);
auto opened = redxai::duress_open("everyday-pass", blob.data(), blob.size(), 8);
```

`redxai::duress_size(band, n_slots)` returns the size. `redxai::DURESS_SLOTS` is 8.

There is no decoy command on the CLI in 0.3.1. Seal with an SDK, write the bytes to a file, then `redsecretsxai convert` that file to a PNG.

# Hardware binding (optional)

> **Public documentation only.** The implementation repository remains private. These guides describe the wiki's 0.3.1 interface; published package versions and platform support may differ. Source-checkout/build steps require separately authorized source access. Examples use dummy credentials.

Hardware binding ties a [port](Port-mode.md) keymap to this machine. The provider is one of: TPM 2.0 on Linux, macOS Keychain (this device only), Windows DPAPI or the TPM platform provider, or a protected token file. A weaker machine-id fallback can be used when none of those are available. Check the provider name before you depend on it.

Losing the machine means you need [key cards](Shamir-key-cards.md) or the [recovery key](Recovery.md). The password alone will not open that keymap on a different computer.

## See which provider this machine has

```bash
redsecretsxai hw-token
```

Stdout is 32 bytes of token, hex encoded. Stderr names the provider and prints a strength note. `RSX_WARN_HW_WEAK` means the machine-id fallback: it binds to this machine and does not add copy protection. The note from `hw_strength_note` says the same thing when the strength is weak.

```python
from redxai import _librsx as rsx
token, flags = rsx.hw_token_ex()
print(rsx.hw_provider_name())
print(rsx.hw_strength_note())
print("weak fallback:", bool(flags & rsx.RSX_WARN_HW_WEAK))
```

```csharp
using RedXAI.Encrypt;
var hw = RedSecret.HwTokenEx();
Console.WriteLine(RedSecret.HwProviderName());
Console.WriteLine(RedSecret.HwProviderStrength());
Console.WriteLine(hw.Weak ? "machine-id fallback" : "sealed provider");
```

```java
import com.redxai.encrypt.RedSecret;
RedSecret.HwToken hw = RedSecret.hwTokenEx();
System.out.println(RedSecret.hwProviderName());
System.out.println(RedSecret.hwProviderStrength());
System.out.println(hw.weak() ? "machine-id fallback" : "sealed provider");
```

```cpp
#include <redxai/encrypt.hpp>
#include <iostream>
auto h = redxai::hw_token_ex();
std::cout << redxai::hw_provider_name() << "\n";
std::cout << redxai::hw_provider_strength() << "\n";
if (h.weak()) std::cout << "machine-id fallback\n";
```

The JavaScript package implements `hwTokenEx` inside its native helper, and the public `require('red-xai-encrypt')` entry does not export it in 0.3.1. Use the CLI or another SDK to read the provider.

## Create a hardware-bound keymap (Python)

```python
from redxai import _librsx as rsx

s, warn = rsx.session_create_hw(b"pw-2026", "laptop-port/session.kxai")
print("bound via", rsx.hw_provider_name(), "warn", warn)
s.close()

s, warn = rsx.session_open_hw(b"pw-2026", "laptop-port/session.kxai")
s.close()
```

Pass your own 32-byte token only if you already have one. Omit it to use the provider above.

C++ can call the same core function:

```cpp
#include <redxai/encrypt.hpp>
// rsx.h is included by encrypt.hpp (rsx_session_create_hw, rsx_session_close).

int err = 0;
uint32_t warn = 0;
std::string pw = "pw-2026";
rsx_session *hw = rsx_session_create_hw(
    reinterpret_cast<const uint8_t *>(pw.data()), pw.size(),
    "laptop-port/session.kxai",
    nullptr,  // token; NULL uses this machine's provider
    &warn, &err);
if (!hw) {
  // err is the library code from rsx.h
} else {
  rsx_session_close(hw);
}
```

Open later with `rsx_session_open_hw`. The JavaScript, C#, and Java easy APIs do not wrap create/open of a hardware-bound keymap in 0.3.1. Create it from Python or C++, then those languages can still use a normal port path only if that keymap was not hardware-bound. A hardware-bound `session.kxai` opens with `session_open_hw` / `rsx_session_open_hw`.

# Java

> **Public documentation only.** The implementation repository remains private. These guides describe the wiki's 0.3.1 interface; published package versions and platform support may differ. Source-checkout/build steps require separately authorized source access. Examples use dummy credentials.

JDK 21. Maven coordinate `com.redxai:red-xai-encrypt:0.3.1`.

```bash
java --enable-preview --enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED -cp … YourApp
```

```java
import com.redxai.encrypt.RedSecret;
```

This package writes a **binary** `.Red_SecretXAI`. `toImage`, `toBinary`, `rerender`, `splitImages`, `joinImages`, `splitPortKey`, `restoreFromCards`, `createPort`, and `unlockPortWithRecoveryKey` throw `available in the Python SDK only … until v0.4`. Use [convert](Red-X-PNG.md). A PNG path raises an error that names the Python package. `encryptFolder` also rejects an output path that ends in `.png`.

## Password mode

The two-argument methods use port mode. Pass `"password"` for a self-contained file.

```java
import com.redxai.encrypt.RedSecret;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;

Files.createDirectories(Path.of("restored"));
String out = RedSecret.encryptFile(
    "notes.txt", "correct horse battery staple", "password", null, null, false);
System.out.println(RedSecret.decryptFile(
    out, "correct horse battery staple", "restored", null, null, null, false));

String token = RedSecret.encryptText(
    "hello", "correct horse battery staple", "password", null, null);
System.out.println(RedSecret.decryptText(token, "correct horse battery staple"));
```

`encryptFile` arguments after the password: mode, port path, setup callback, overwrite.  
`decryptFile` arguments after the password: output directory, mode, port path, setup callback, overwrite.

## Errors

`WrongPasswordException`, `NotRedSecretException`, `FileExistsRedXAIException`, `UnsafeFilenameException`, `SetupDeclinedException`, `NativeLibraryException`. All extend `RedXAIException`.

## Optional calls

```java
RedSecret.encryptFile("notes.txt", "pw-2026", "port", "/media/usb/.red-xai-port", () -> true, false);

RedSecret.encryptFolder("photos", "pw-2026", "photos.Red_SecretXAI",
    "port", "/media/usb/.red-xai-port", () -> true, RedSecret.ARCH_COMPRESS, null, null);
Files.createDirectories(Path.of("photos-restored"));
RedSecret.decryptFolder("photos.Red_SecretXAI", "pw-2026", "photos-restored",
    "/media/usb/.red-xai-port", null, 0, null, null);

RedSecret.encryptDrive("disk.img", "pw-2026", "disk.Red_SecretXAI",
    null, () -> true, RedSecret.ARCH_COMPRESS, null, null);
RedSecret.decryptDrive("disk.Red_SecretXAI", "pw-2026", "disk-restored.img",
    null, null, 0, null, null, null);

byte[] blob = RedSecret.duressSeal(
    new String[] {"real-pass", "everyday-pass"},
    new byte[][] {"real".getBytes(), "cover".getBytes()},
    8, 0);
RedSecret.duressOpen("everyday-pass", blob, 8);

var preview = RedSecret.archivePreview("photos.Red_SecretXAI", "pw-2026", "/media/usb/.red-xai-port");
RedSecret.HwToken hw = RedSecret.hwTokenEx();
System.out.println(RedSecret.hwProviderName() + " " + RedSecret.hwProviderStrength());
```

Folder seals must use mode `"port"`. Flags: `ARCH_COMPRESS` (1), `ARCH_ALLOW_SYMLINKS` (2), `ARCH_ALLOW_SETUID` (4), `ARCH_WRITE_DEVICE` (8). Progress is `RedSecret.ProgressFn`. Cancel is an `AtomicBoolean`.

A series of messages, opened in order. Master is 32 bytes. Salt is 16 bytes or null.

```java
import com.redxai.encrypt.Ratchet;
byte[] master = new byte[32];
byte[] salt = new byte[16];
java.security.SecureRandom.getInstanceStrong().nextBytes(master);
java.security.SecureRandom.getInstanceStrong().nextBytes(salt);
try (Ratchet tx = new Ratchet(master, salt); Ratchet rx = new Ratchet(master, salt)) {
    byte[] frame = tx.seal("frame 0".getBytes());
    System.out.println(new String(rx.open(frame)));
}
```

More: [Port mode](Port-mode.md), [Folders, apps, and drives](Folders-apps-and-drives.md), [Hardware binding](Hardware-binding.md), [Decoy passwords](Decoy-passwords.md).

# JavaScript

> **Public documentation only.** The implementation repository remains private. These guides describe the wiki's 0.3.1 interface; published package versions and platform support may differ. Source-checkout/build steps require separately authorized source access. Examples use dummy credentials.

`npm install red-xai-encrypt` on Node 18+. If install scripts are blocked: `npm install-scripts approve koffi`.

```js
const R = require('red-xai-encrypt');
```

This package writes a **binary** `.Red_SecretXAI`. It does not write or read a red-X PNG in 0.3.1. `toImage`, `toBinary`, `rerender`, `splitImages`, `joinImages`, `splitPortKey`, `restoreFromCards`, `createPort`, and `unlockPort` throw `available in the Python SDK only … until v0.4`. Use the [CLI](Red-X-PNG.md) for the PNG step. Opening a PNG throws an error that names the Python package.

## Password mode

```js
const R = require('red-xai-encrypt');
const fs = require('fs');

fs.mkdirSync('restored', { recursive: true });
const out = R.encryptFile('notes.txt', 'correct horse battery staple', { mode: 'password' });
console.log(R.decryptFile(out, 'correct horse battery staple', { outDir: 'restored' }));

const token = R.encryptText('hello', 'correct horse battery staple', { mode: 'password' });
console.log(R.decryptText(token, 'correct horse battery staple'));
```

Then:

```bash
redsecretsxai convert notes.txt.Red_SecretXAI notes.png
```

## Errors

`WrongPasswordError`, `NotRedSecretError`, `FileExistsRedXAIError`, `UnsafeFilenameError`, `SetupDeclinedError`, `NativeLibraryError`. All extend `RedXAIError`.

```js
try {
  R.decryptText(token, 'wrong');
} catch (e) {
  if (e instanceof R.WrongPasswordError) console.log('wrong password or the file was changed');
}
```

Pass `{ overwrite: true }` when the `.Red_SecretXAI` is already there and you mean to replace that output. The library still will not replace an unrelated existing file on decrypt unless you set overwrite.

## Optional calls

```js
R.encryptFile('notes.txt', 'pw-2026', {
  mode: 'port',
  portPath: '/media/usb/.red-xai-port',
  onSetupPrompt: () => true,
});

R.encryptFolder('photos', 'pw-2026', 'photos.Red_SecretXAI', { onSetupPrompt: () => true });
R.decryptFolder('photos.Red_SecretXAI', 'pw-2026', 'photos-restored');

R.encryptDrive('disk.img', 'pw-2026', 'disk.Red_SecretXAI', { onSetupPrompt: () => true });
R.decryptDrive('disk.Red_SecretXAI', 'pw-2026', 'disk-restored.img');

const blob = R.duressSeal(
  ['real-pass', 'everyday-pass'],
  [Buffer.from('real'), Buffer.from('cover')],
  { slots: 8, band: 0 },
);
R.duressOpen('everyday-pass', blob, { slots: 8 });

const { entries, summary } = R.archivePreview('photos.Red_SecretXAI', 'pw-2026');
```

Folder and drive calls always use a port. `output: 'image'` throws. Flags: `1` compress, `2` symlinks, `4` setuid, `8` write device. Device restore also needs `confirmDeviceName` set to that same path.

Progress and cancel:

```js
const ac = new AbortController();
R.encryptFolder('big', 'pw-2026', 'big.Red_SecretXAI', {
  onSetupPrompt: () => true,
  progress: (done, total) => { if (done > 5_000_000) ac.abort(); },
  signal: ac.signal,
});
```

A series of buffers, opened in the same order, with the same 32-byte master and 16-byte salt:

```js
const crypto = require('crypto');
const master = crypto.randomBytes(32);
const salt = crypto.randomBytes(16);
const tx = new R.Ratchet(master, salt);
const rx = new R.Ratchet(master, salt);
const frame = tx.seal(Buffer.from('frame 0'));
console.log(rx.open(frame).toString());
tx.close();
rx.close();
```

`FILE_EXT` is `.Red_SecretXAI`. `TEXT_PREFIX` is `RXAI2.`.

More: [Port mode](Port-mode.md), [Folders, apps, and drives](Folders-apps-and-drives.md), [Decoy passwords](Decoy-passwords.md).

# Seal and open a file (password mode)

> **Public documentation only.** The implementation repository remains private. These guides describe the wiki's 0.3.1 interface; published package versions and platform support may differ. Source-checkout/build steps require separately authorized source access. Examples use dummy credentials.

Password mode is the path to start with. The sealed file carries what the password needs. You do not create a port, and you do not keep a second folder.

The output name is always the input name plus `.Red_SecretXAI`. Existing outputs are left alone unless you pass the overwrite flag.

Then do the [red-X PNG step](Red-X-PNG.md). Python already writes that PNG. The other SDKs write a binary container, and `convert` turns it into the PNG you send.

## Python

```python
from redxai import encrypt_file, decrypt_file
import os

os.makedirs("restored", exist_ok=True)
out = encrypt_file("notes.txt", "correct horse battery staple", mode="password")
print("sealed:", out)   # notes.txt.Red_SecretXAI, a red-X PNG
print("restored:", decrypt_file(out, "correct horse battery staple", out_dir="restored"))
```

`output="image"` is the default. Pass `output="binary"` when you want a raw `.Red_SecretXAI` to hand to `convert` yourself:

```python
raw = encrypt_file("notes.txt", "correct horse battery staple", mode="password", output="binary")
```

## JavaScript

```js
const R = require('red-xai-encrypt');
const fs = require('fs');

fs.mkdirSync('restored', { recursive: true });
const out = R.encryptFile('notes.txt', 'correct horse battery staple', { mode: 'password' });
console.log('sealed:', out);   // notes.txt.Red_SecretXAI, binary container
console.log('restored:', R.decryptFile(out, 'correct horse battery staple', { outDir: 'restored' }));
```

`output: 'image'` throws in 0.3.1. Convert the binary file with the [CLI](Red-X-PNG.md) (Python) before you send it.

## C#

```csharp
using RedXAI.Encrypt;

Directory.CreateDirectory("restored");
var outPath = RedSecret.EncryptFile("notes.txt", "correct horse battery staple", mode: "password");
Console.WriteLine("sealed: " + outPath);
Console.WriteLine("restored: " + RedSecret.DecryptFile(outPath, "correct horse battery staple", outDir: "restored"));
```

This writes a binary `.Red_SecretXAI`. `ToImage` throws until a later version. Use [convert](Red-X-PNG.md).

## Java

```java
import com.redxai.encrypt.RedSecret;

java.nio.file.Files.createDirectories(java.nio.file.Path.of("restored"));
String out = RedSecret.encryptFile(
    "notes.txt", "correct horse battery staple", "password", null, null, false);
System.out.println("sealed: " + out);
System.out.println("restored: " + RedSecret.decryptFile(
    out, "correct horse battery staple", "restored", null, null, null, false));
```

The two-argument form `encryptFile(path, password)` is port mode, not password mode. Pass `"password"` as the mode argument, as above. The file is binary. Use [convert](Red-X-PNG.md) for the PNG.

Run with `--enable-preview --enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED`.

